            <ArticleTitle>Fewer Seniors from United States Allopathic Medical Schools are Filling Pathology Residency Positions in the Main Residency Match, 2008-2017.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0046-8177(17)30433-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.humpath.2017.11.014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Some pathologists have observed that fewer American medical school trainees are entering pathology residency. This trend was measured and further explored using Main Residency Match (MRM) data from 2008 to 2017, obtained from the National Resident Matching Program (NRMP). Over the past decade, the proportion of pathology residency positions filled in the MRM which were taken by American medical school trainees decreased from 77.7% to 50.1%. This was primarily due to fewer seniors from United States (US) allopathic medical schools filling pathology positions in the MRM (298 in 2008 vs 216 in 2017, a 27.5% decrease). Compared to 14 other medical specialties, pathology had the largest decline in the proportion of pathology positions filled in the MRM which were taken by seniors from US allopathic medical schools (63.8% in 2008 vs 39.6% in 2017). The primary reason for this decline was because fewer seniors from US allopathic medical schools participated in the MRM for pathology positions (326 in 2008 vs 232 in 2017, a 28.8% decrease), however, the underlying reasons for this decline are unknown. In conclusion, over the past decade, fewer seniors from US allopathic medical schools sought / filled pathology residency positions in the MRM. These findings are relevant for pathology residency recruitment, especially in the context of a projected decline in US pathologist workforce.</AbstractText>
